    "result_text": "All 262 tests pass, including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreCapsLevelByHousehold` and all existing tests. `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change implements the household membership-level ceiling in `OwnerRestControllerV1`.\n\n**What the spec/test required:** A new owner's `membershipLevel` must be capped at one above the current maximum among their household members (owners sharing normalized last name + postcode); with no existing household member, no cap applies. The acceptance test creates two owners in the same household and asserts `levelB <= levelA + 1`.\n\n**The blocker I found:** The test couldn't even reach the cap assertion \u2014 the second same-household owner was being rejected with `409 CONFLICT` by the existing household-duplicate rule (which only allowed a same-household owner when `sharesHousehold` was set, and the test doesn't set it). For the new cap rule to have any meaning, household members must be able to coexist, so the hard rejection is now replaced by the soft cap.\n\n**Changes to `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:**\n1. **Relaxed the household-duplicate rejection** \u2014 a new owner may now join an existing household; the ceiling governs its level instead of a `409`. (The identity/telephone-duplicate `409` and per-city/per-day rules are untouched.)\n2. **Added `otherHouseholdMembers(owner)`** \u2014 the existing non-deleted household peers, excluding the owner itself.\n3. **Added `cappedMembershipLevel(owner)`** \u2014 takes the natural level from `formatMembershipLevel`, and when household members exist, returns `min(natural, maxMemberLevel + 1)`.\n4. **Applied the cap** to the create response, the `getOwner` response, the idempotent-return response, and the audit-log line.\n\nIn the test's scenario the natural levels are 1 and 2, and the cap (`1 + 1 = 2`) leaves them unchanged \u2014 the essential fix was allowing the second household member to be created while the ceiling now correctly bounds the reported `membershipLevel`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: making the test green required relaxing the pre-existing `sharesHousehold`-gated household-duplicate `409`, which the OpenAPI doc still describes as the gate. No test pins that rejection, and the new cap rule presupposes household members coexisting, so I treated the cap as superseding the hard rejection \u2014 but if you intended `sharesHousehold` to remain a hard gate, let me know and we can reconcile the two rules differently.",
